Day 4 of My DevOps Real-Time Scenario Series
Scenario: Jenkins Pipeline Succeeds, But the Application Isn't Updated

A developer confirms that the latest code has been merged into the `main` branch.

The Jenkins pipeline completes successfully:
✅ Code cloned
✅ Maven build passed
✅ Docker image built
✅ Image pushed to the registry
✅ Deployment completed

But the QA team still sees the old version of the application.

As a DevOps Engineer, what would you check first?

Here's the troubleshooting approach I would follow:

🔹 Verify Jenkins built the latest Git commit.
🔹 Confirm the new Docker image exists in the registry.
🔹 Check whether Kubernetes/ECS deployed the latest image.
🔹 Verify the image running inside the pod or container.
🔹 Review the `imagePullPolicy` configuration.
🔹 Restart the deployment if required.
🔹 Validate the application version inside the running container.
🔹 Check for browser or CDN caching issues.
🔹 Ensure the Load Balancer is routing traffic to the updated instances.

💡 One common root cause:

Using the `latest` image tag with `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent`.

In this case, Kubernetes may continue using the cached image instead of pulling the newly built one.

Best Practice:
✔️ Use unique image tags (such as the Git commit SHA or build number).
✔️ Avoid relying on the `latest` tag in production.
✔️ Verify the deployed version after every release.
